#Introduction
Platforms in the gaming industry have evolved drastically over many decades where things like conenience and accessibility to games have improved and changed the way people interact with and use gaming technology. However the evolution of systems and jumps to new technology to access gaming have created ethical challenges with consumer rights, accessibility and privacy.

Digital game ownership has been a revolutionary advancement in the industry which allowed users to access games without a physical disc or cartridge copy through an online service. This service and the players access to the product is fully dependant on online services which creates a lot of controversy around ownership and rights. There is also the issue with bugs that require fixing within games that can only be maintained through updates from an online service, this does not guarantee people who rely on their physical copy permanent access to all the features that should be included. The European commission recognieses that certain games and online gaming services will become unusable if a game company decides to shut their servers down, this has been a big concern to customers who want lasting value from their gaming purchases.




